{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "The code is disposable and the only thing that matters is how fast you learn whether the idea is worth pursuing, so anything that slows the loop is waste. Keep it walled off from real data, credentials, and customers, and delete it rather than promoting it."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo has to survive contact with an audience, so you need to understand and be able to fix the parts you will actually show. Review selectively and test the demo path, and assume someone will want to promote this code: treat anything you would hate to inherit as still prototype-grade."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Real users mean real consequences, but speed still dominates and most MVP code is low-blast-radius UI and glue. Review selectively and check behavior with tests, with mandatory line-by-line review for anything touching auth, payments, PII, infrastructure, or destructive operations."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Paying customers and an on-call team turn unread AI code into a liability, because you cannot secure, debug, or maintain what nobody understands. Treat AI output like a stranger's pull request: every line read by someone accountable, backed by tests, CI, and monitoring."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "Regulated and safety-critical systems require demonstrated human understanding, traceability, and accountability for each line, which generate-and-accept cannot provide. Let AI draft, explain, and critique, but a qualified engineer must write or rewrite the code of record."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Vibe coding buys speed by spending future understanding, so spend it where the future is short. Tighten as the cost of being wrong rises: level 4 only for code you will throw away, level 3 once stakeholders or real users depend on it, level 2 once customers and long-term maintenance do, and level 1 when a human has to sign their name to every line. Two rules hold at every level: never accept code you could not debug if it broke tonight, and never let unreviewed code touch credentials, payments, PII, or production infrastructure."
}
